Z||000016||danny boy||04-20-2001||12:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR>[B]it's  96+ accord.. it's mis-identified. WOW! thats impressive.. squished like a beer can!  guess you had to be there but how can you POSSIBLY not see a BIG A$$ trailer!! <P>oh well Darwin at work...  <BR> <P>[ B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> You would be surprised. I had a girl drive under me in a parking lot in PA one time. said she didn't know I was turning. I figured the 5 signals down the side of my truck and the big "this truck makes wide right turns" sign would have given it away. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spektyr||04-20-2001||01:35 AM||||I put this right up with people that get hit by cars while crossing the street.<P>"But I didn't see him coming!"<P>What part didn't you notice, the stepping into the street (where cars drive) or the one ton (or more) vehicle barreling down the street?<P>And I know for damn sure I can jump across an entire lane of traffic if properly motivated (ie all-too rapidly approching vehicle grille).<P>What's really sad is when one of these vehicular idiots manages to take out a normal person while they're intently studying their car stereo face plate, reaching for the fudgesicle they dropped under their seat, or god knows what other stupid thing they did while driving.<P>I mean, two retards slamming into each other is funny.  One moron taking out a minivan full of kids because he was trying to get his passenger side door completely closed is sad.<P>(Well, not sad because of the minivan, but the kids.)<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||1
Z||000018||fierohoho||04-20-2001||02:11 AM||||If I only had a flatbed scanner I could show you some wreck photos that would make you believe in luck.||||reg||1
Z||000019||stsmithgt||04-20-2001||09:48 AM||||Back in the 70's when I was a kid, my dad owned two Shelbys.  He was a Deputy Sheriff in southern California.  I liked his cars while growing up so much, he was afraid I'd get some HOT ROD and end up killing myself in it.  His plan was to show me several photos he has taken at crash sites he had to go to.  I can see where he was coming from though I didn't know it at the time.  Of course the first car I got had an engine out of a dirt track sprint car in it. he he. 377 c.i. 11.5:1 ratio popup pistons.(found that out after I blew it up.) I've overworked my guardian angel through the years that's for sure. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Homicidal||04-20-2001||02:55 PM||||Bah, racing is SO retarded.  If you wanna drag, go to the salt flats or to a track, but don't endanger other people on the road because you need that sense of power.<P>I always get the Rice Rockets teasing me, revving their engines and getting ready to race.  I always rev back, laugh and smile.  When the light turns green, they waste their gas and tread and peel out, speeding away.  I just accelerate normally and laugh when the cop pulls them over  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>I drive the speed limit, life's too short to speed through it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Nashco||04-21-2001||03:58 AM||||Life is too short, I agree, so why waste time worrying about things like tire tread and fuel when you're trying to have a good time.  Now, I don't support idiots driving like asses, don't get me wrong.  I believe there's a time and place for everything, and situations where there's a minivan nearby, or in crowded streets, is not the place...but there are plenty of places that are fine for street racing.  I may be of the "invincible kid" mind...but it is possible to drive "good" (normal..easy...law abidingly) most of the time and aggressive others.  I have had my fair share of, "holy sh*t...that was intense" moments, but have failed to ever get a speeding ticket or in an accident by following this policy.  Remeber that folks, there is a time and place for everything.  I don't think we should consider all street racing stupid...only stupid (hazardous, threatening) street racing.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000022||AusFiero||04-21-2001||08:22 PM||||As a lucky survivor of my youth I will agree that high speed street racing is not worth it. Too many unknown variables and too many other people to hurt. Organised events are more fun anyway as you get to see what it really goes like. <P>Not that I am angel mind you, I still like to have a little go at the lights in areas I know people aren't around. Just race them to the speed limit. Is usually only a 2nd to 3rg gear  tussle and ya made ya point by then anyway. Let them get the ticket if they want to then fly by and think theyv'e won. <P>We also have many windy mountain roads 10 minutes drive away. They can be fun just trying to do the speed limits.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||04-21-2001||09:23 PM||||I don't (didn't?) know the guy in the white honda, so I can't comment. I don't know what he/she was doing at the time. It's a little tasteless to trivialize a human life based entirely on the car he/she drives. I know a lot of stupid people who have are very squirrely* about their little ricers, and love to street race. As retarded as I think the cars look, and as obnoxious I find their exhaust notes, having a rice rocket is not a crime punishable by death. <P>To be honsest, it would appear to me (based on an accident my friend was in) that this person was tryin to pass someone on the curbside lane, and didn't back down. No responsiblity rests on anyone but the driver, but it is still a tragic loss. Unless anyone can prove to me that this person truly did deserve to die, I am saddened at the loss, and hope that the family doesn't EVER see this thread. For shame  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>I will agree that speed inside the city is stupid. I have always been a fan of "race to the [speed] limit" and have never gone past 20kph over during a race. I don't mind goofing off in the city at night with all the sunday-night cruisers. I don't EVER get my car past 70kph on Portage, and I ***NEVER*** race within 2 blocks of a pedestrian x-walk. <P>*Squirrely: finds great value in a worthless object.||||reg||9
Z||000024||Seanh||04-21-2001||11:15 PM||||Well, I just say there is a huge difference between driving fast and driving irresponsibly. I race the ones every weekend. Speed limit is 35, I race up to about 50 and then back off, of couse they continue up to like 90 and then brake hard. I relize when I race someone, I can only be sure of myself. I feel confident knowing how my car handles and the limits I can push it to. But, I guess if I race someone, and they have no idea how to drive and ends up crashing and killing themselves or someone else it would be somewhat my fault. I know I can drive fast and still be a safe driver. I also know someone else could drive 10 mph and kill a whole bus load of kids. I dunnno which side I'm really on.||||reg||1
Z||000025||firstfiero||04-22-2001||01:12 PM||||    exactly....I street race allthe time. But don't confuse me with an idiot in a honda who thinks its cool to go 100+ on city streets. I save those speeds for open highways. I run her up to 50 or 60 at the most but back down from there. If I didn't wanted to drive fast I would have bought a 4 banger fiero... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||4
Z||000026||Spektyr||04-22-2001||02:23 PM||||The one philosphy that will save your life more than any other:<P>Assume that everyone else on the road is a complete moron, and figure out what the dumbest possible thing they could do is.  Prepare for that.  If they actually do it, you won't be surprised.  If they don't, just wait... you'll find the real moron eventually.<P>I love to race.  I'm not souping up my Fiero to win car shows, because I think it will sound cool, or because I have nothing better to spend my money on.  I'm doing it because I want to go insanely fast, and get to that speed very quickly.<P>That being said, I don't know ANYONE quicker on the brakes than me.  Every person I've ridden with starts stopping later than me, and follows cars closer than me.  I've been in over a dozen car wrecks, and been the driver in over half of them.  I've been T-boned by a '79 Caprice doing 50mph.  I've spent 3 weeks in physical therapy so that I could sit up without screaming.<P>And I've learned.  You show me an open road, I'll show you an open throttle, but only for a little bit.  I have my fun, I downshift, gear brake and then assume a more reasonable speed.  I'll cruise at about 120% of the posted limit, and shoot up to about 150-160% and drop back down.  But if I have ANY reason to think I can't dodge or stop short of any other car on the road, I get the hell out of the way.  The way I see it I've been in the middle of twisted steel enough times, and walked away from enough accidents that should've killed me, my luck is used up.  The next time I'm probably rolling loaded dice.<P>Know what's around you.  Predict the worst that can happen.  If it involves a painful vehicular 'whoops', keep your foot off the gas.  If not, have fun.  And if you've absolutely gotta push the limits of the car, find a track.  It's the only way to do it and not be certifiably stupid.  I've seen a Mustang hit a brick at 135mph.  You can't see a brick in time at that speed.  He had a rollcage and walked away.  Do you?<P>In short, I never do anything I'm not prepared to pay the consequences for.  I don't speed more than I can afford the ticket for, never race faster than I think I can survive a crash, and never gamble anything I can't afford to lose.  It doesn't mean I don't have fun.  It just means I don't have TOO much fun.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000027||Mach10||04-22-2001||05:50 PM||||Don't get me wrong. I LOVE street racing. But I don't push it. I don't keep the gas down much past the speed limit. If I want to go fast, I leave the city and head to the open straights. <P>My point is that unless someone can tell me without a shred of doubt that this person was  speeding, racing, or driving irresponsibly, then this person should be treated with a little more dignity. Whatever the cause of the accident, he/she has paid for it many times over, and the family is probably still paying.<P><BR>Neat thing is that if you ask ANYONE that drives a car to rate their skill, most (I believe the statistical norm is 80%) will say better than average. It's hard to come to grips with. I know my limits and I try and keep an eye out around me. Until I have some auto-cross trophies on my shelf, I will refuse to comment on my own skill.||||reg||9
Z||000028||Spektyr||04-22-2001||06:07 PM||||Mach, I agree with what you said about what people rate their driving skill at.  Of course there's a few things to think about on that.<P>Women:  Not to use stereotypes or anything but the majority of women don't think of driving skill as the ability to do amazing things with a car.  They tend to think of it as the likelyhood of them successfully navigating an automobile from point A to point B without breaking the law or getting into a wreck.  Most likely if a woman feels they have above average skill, they do, simply because they have a 'safer' set of criteria.<P>Men: Well, we all know that men think of it in terms of ability to outrun, outdrive and generally outdo another driver.  A fast car with a 'good' driver can outrun cops (but not the radio).  A 'good' driver can make a 40mph corner swinging the tail end wide and never break a sweat.  A 'great' driver can race in the big leagues, NASCAR, Indy, etc.  Now every male has an opinion of what his skill is.  This can be inflated by false confidence, but when it comes to the answer to the "How do you rate your driving skill" there's much more to consider than simply that man's self-image.  There's his perception of other people's perception of him.  We're men, we're supposed to be able to chop down trees, bench press our weight, start a fire by rubbing sticks together, and drive better than our buddies.  It wouldn't be 'manly' to say "I need to learn more".<P>So yeah, probably 95% of men would rate their skill at 'Above Average' or higher.  But I'd say half the ones that lie know they're lying.<P>I'd say I'm above average... I've got some street racing experience.  I've got a bit of autocross experience.  But you put me and a 'good' driver in identical cars... I dunno.  I've got more to learn.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>With any luck I won't have to learn any more lessons with shrieking metal and bursting glass as background music.<BR><P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000029||Mach10||04-22-2001||07:08 PM||||Very true, Spektyr<BR>Another point to add:<BR>SENIORS. Our news people did an article, and it was interesting to note that all of the seniors interviewed considered themselves above average to EXPERT drivers. When the news vans were following them around, watching them do really STUPID things that they were not aware of. My hat goes off in respect to the people who built our countries, and will freely admit that very few people are actually all that bad, but when you are drifting back and forth across lanes, and making right turns from the left hand lane, WITHOUT realizing it, I'm sorry. Younger drivers are not at fault. Get off the road before you kill someone. Nobody around you is speeding. It only looks that way because you are going 60kph on the TransCanada. Just the other day, I saw a car full of seniors ATTEMPT to run a yellow. The lincoln WAS pretty fast. went from 35kph (in a 60) to about 70 in 3-4 seconds. Too bad that pedestrian jogger (I watched this before the incoming Linky. VERY fine jogger  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) was in the way. The Linky slams it's breaks, WAY too late, but the jogger had already dived out of the way. Way too close, and good argument for mandatory testing.<P>Actually, I say forget mandatory testing for those above 60. I say test EVERYBODY every 5 years. That'll clean things up.||||reg||6
Z||000030||Spektyr||04-22-2001||07:14 PM||||How about graded licenses?<P>An "A" grade license says you're a kick-butt driver, "B" not quite so good, etc.<P>That way you could PROVE just how good you are without trying to beat some other retard to a light and smash into the back of a semi...<P>Just a thought...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000031||Mach10||04-22-2001||07:34 PM||||We already have that. It's called Merit Points. If you have a good track-record for 5 years (no traffic violations/hiway code, no at-fault accidents) then you get 5 merits. These are good for insurance rate cuts (our insurance is government owned monopoly, and tied with Dept. Motor Vehicles). All it means  is that you haven't been caught.<BR>Our government wants to implement a graduated licence system for new drivers, imposing restrictions at first, and slackening off after X years of good driving. Good idea, but the current implementation would be little bass-ackward.||||reg||1
